SukiSU/SUSFS: wire ksu_handle_setresuid into kernel/sys.c

With CONFIG_KSU_SUSFS=y the driver's LSM task_fix_setuid hook is
compiled out (lsm_hook.c guards it with #ifndef CONFIG_KSU_SUSFS), and
ksu_handle_setresuid() becomes the only path that installs the manager
driver fd (ksu_install_fd) and disables seccomp for the manager. Nothing
in this kernel called it, so the SukiSU manager always reports
"no KernelSU driver detected on kernel" even though the driver is
present and the kernel otherwise works.

Wiring taken from WildKernels/Sultan_KernelSU_SUSFS (same 5.4 vendor
tree family): declare the handler under CONFIG_KSU_SUSFS right before
__sys_setresuid() and call it at the top of the function body. The
handler itself filters by zygote SID, so calling it on every setresuid
is safe and matches the reference implementation.
---
diff --git a/kernel/sys.c b/kernel/sys.c
--- a/kernel/sys.c
+++ b/kernel/sys.c
@@ -617,6 +617,10 @@
 	return __sys_setuid(uid);
 }
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_KSU_SUSFS
+extern int ksu_handle_setresuid(uid_t ruid, uid_t euid, uid_t suid);
+#endif
+
 
 /*
  * This function implements a generic ability to update ruid, euid,
@@ -630,6 +634,9 @@
 	int retval;
 	kuid_t kruid, keuid, ksuid;
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_KSU_SUSFS
+	(void)ksu_handle_setresuid(ruid, euid, suid);
+#endif
 	kruid = make_kuid(ns, ruid);
 	keuid = make_kuid(ns, euid);
 	ksuid = make_kuid(ns, suid);
